---
import Base from '../../../layouts/Base.astro';
import Section from '../../../components/Section.astro';
import Prose from '../../../components/Prose.astro';
import { veil } from '../../../data/veil';

/**
 * Veil's app privacy policy — the App Store Connect Privacy Policy URL
 * (website decision 0010, extending 0009's support-page exception). App Review
 * and users read it as a legal statement, so every claim is checked against
 * the app's source (ProjectPrivacy, develop @ 8444297, 2026-11-16):
 *   · OCR is Apple's Vision framework; detection (Lite rules, or the GLiNER
 *     model on the GPU) and redaction run in-process; no server of its own;
 *   · the vault: AES-GCM, a 256-bit key in the keychain as
 *     WhenUnlockedThisDeviceOnly, files written with complete file protection,
 *     directory excluded from backup (SecureStorageDirectoryProvider); the
 *     model snapshot is excluded too (ModelSnapshotBackupExcluder);
 *   · the only network code is OnDeviceModelDownloader: HEAD + ranged GETs of
 *     five public files of fastino/gliner2-base-v1 from huggingface.co, no
 *     custom headers, only when the on-device engine is chosen (~800 MB);
 *   · feedback: FeedbackMessageFactory can only see bundle, device, settings
 *     and the GPU probe; it hands a mailto: to the system (SendFeedbackUseCase);
 *   · export: RedactionExporter clears PDF documentAttributes (PP-85); share
 *     stagings in tmp/ are swept on the next launch;
 *   · purchase: StoreKit, product veil.pro.lifetime; rating prompt is the
 *     system requestReview.
 * The contact address must match FeedbackContact.address in the app and the
 * support page. Re-verify all of this before editing the copy, and move the
 * effective date with any change of substance.
 */

const email = 'rezabina.dev@gmail.com';
const effective = { iso: '2026-11-16', label: '16 November 2026' };

const title = `${veil.name} privacy policy · Reza Bina`;
const description =
  'The privacy policy for Veil, the iPhone app. Documents are processed and stored on the phone; the app has no accounts, analytics, ads or server of its own.';

const site = Astro.site!.href;
const breadcrumb = {
  '@type': 'BreadcrumbList',
  itemListElement: [
    { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 1, name: 'Home', item: site },
    { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 2, name: 'Apps', item: `${site}#apps` },
    { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 3, name: veil.name, item: new URL('/work/veil/', site).href },
    { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 4, name: 'Privacy policy' },
  ],
};
---

<Base title={title} description={description} ogSlug="work-veil" schema={breadcrumb}>
  <article class="wrap policy">
    <header class="policy__head">
      <h1 class="policy__title">Veil privacy policy</h1>
      <p class="policy__meta">
        Effective <time datetime={effective.iso}>{effective.label}</time> ·
        <a href="/work/veil">about the app</a> · <a href="/work/veil/support">support</a>
      </p>
    </header>

    <Section label="in short" id="summary">
      <Prose>
        <p>
          Veil does not collect any data about you. Your documents are read, checked for personal
          details, redacted and stored on your iPhone, and they are never uploaded anywhere. Veil
          has no accounts, no analytics, no advertising, no tracking and no server of its own.
        </p>
        <p>
          This policy covers the Veil app for iPhone, published on the App Store by me, Reza Bina,
          an independent developer in Berlin, Germany. This website is separate and has
          <a href="/privacy">its own privacy page</a>.
        </p>
      </Prose>
    </Section>

    <Section label="your documents" id="documents">
      <Prose>
        <p>
          When you scan a document with the camera, pick one from your photos, or open a PDF or
          image in Veil from another app, everything that happens to it happens on the device. The
          text is recognised with Apple’s on-device text recognition. Personal details are found
          either by the built-in Lite engine or by the on-device AI model, and the redaction is
          drawn on the phone too. Neither engine sends your document, or anything taken from it,
          over the network.
        </p>
        <p>
          Documents you save stay in Veil’s vault on that iPhone. The vault is encrypted with a key
          that is created on the device, kept in its keychain and never leaves it. The vault is
          also excluded from iCloud and computer backups on purpose, so your saved documents do not
          end up in a backup on Apple’s servers or on another machine.
        </p>
      </Prose>
    </Section>

    <Section label="camera and photos" id="camera-and-photos">
      <Prose>
        <p>
          Veil asks for the camera only so you can scan a document, and for your photo library only
          so you can choose a document to redact. It reads just the pictures you capture or pick,
          processes them on the device, and does nothing else with either permission. You can turn
          both off at any time in the iPhone’s Settings app.
        </p>
      </Prose>
    </Section>

    <Section label="the one download" id="model-download">
      <Prose>
        <p>
          The only network connection Veil makes on its own is to download the on-device detection
          model, about 800 MB, and only if you choose the on-device engine. The Lite engine needs no
          download at all. The model is a set of public files fetched from Hugging Face
          (huggingface.co and its content delivery network), and once they are on your phone Veil
          does not download them again unless they are removed.
        </p>
        <p>
          Those requests ask for the model files and nothing more. They contain no document, no
          detected detail, no identifier for you or your device, and nothing about how you use the
          app. Like any connection to a website, they do reveal your IP address and standard
          technical details (such as the iOS networking version) to Hugging Face, which handles
          them under <a href="https://huggingface.co/privacy" rel="noopener">its own privacy
          policy</a>. I receive nothing from these downloads. The model is also excluded from
          backups, so a restored phone downloads it again rather than copying it.
        </p>
      </Prose>
    </Section>

    <Section label="purchases" id="purchases">
      <Prose>
        <p>
          Veil Pro is a one-time in-app purchase made through Apple’s App Store. Apple handles the
          payment and your Apple ID. Veil only learns from Apple whether Pro is unlocked on your
          account, and I never see your name, payment details or Apple ID. Apple’s own privacy
          policy covers the purchase itself, and the same goes for the occasional system prompt
          that asks you to rate the app.
        </p>
      </Prose>
    </Section>

    <Section label="feedback" id="feedback">
      <Prose>
        <p>
          The “Something wasn’t detected?” button opens a new email in your own mail app, addressed
          to me. It is pre-filled with a short prompt and four lines about your setup: the Veil
          version, the iOS version, the device model and the detection engine in use. The part of
          the app that writes that email has no access to your documents, so nothing from them can
          end up in it. You see the whole message before anything is sent, and nothing is sent
          unless you press send yourself.
        </p>
        <p>
          If you do write to me, by that button or directly, I receive your email address and
          whatever you chose to write. I use it only to reply and to improve detection, I never
          share it or add you to any list, and I will delete the conversation if you ask. Please
          describe a missed detail rather than sending it or attaching the document.
        </p>
      </Prose>
    </Section>

    <Section label="sharing a redacted copy" id="sharing">
      <Prose>
        <p>
          When you share or save a redacted copy, it goes only where you send it through the iPhone’s
          share sheet, such as Files, Mail or an AI assistant. Veil strips the document information
          (author, title, creator and similar fields) from exported PDFs, and the temporary copy it
          prepares for sharing is removed the next time the app starts. Once the copy reaches
          another app or service, that service’s own privacy policy applies to it.
        </p>
      </Prose>
    </Section>

    <Section label="keeping and deleting" id="deletion">
      <Prose>
        <p>
          Veil keeps your documents for as long as you keep them in the vault, and you can delete
          any of them in the app at any time. Deleting the app deletes the vault, your settings and
          the downloaded model. iOS may keep the vault’s encryption key in the device keychain
          afterwards, but it is a random key with nothing left to unlock. Because nothing is stored
          anywhere but your phone, there is no copy that I or anyone else could recover, and
          nothing about you for me to export or delete on request beyond any email you sent me.
        </p>
      </Prose>
    </Section>

    <Section label="children" id="children">
      <Prose>
        <p>
          Veil is not directed at children, and it collects no personal information from anyone,
          children included.
        </p>
      </Prose>
    </Section>

    <Section label="changes" id="changes">
      <Prose>
        <p>
          If what Veil does with data ever changes, this page changes before that version of the
          app is released, and the effective date at the top moves with it.
        </p>
      </Prose>
    </Section>

    <Section label="contact" id="contact">
      <Prose>
        <p>
          Questions about this policy or about your data are welcome at
          <a href={`mailto:${email}`}>{email}</a>, the same address as the feedback button in the app.
        </p>
      </Prose>
    </Section>
  </article>
</Base>

<style>
  .policy__head {
    /* A page title is a bigger event than a section: 56, not 40. */
    margin-top: 56px;
  }

  /* A page title: weight and colour, not a section label — no # marker. */
  .policy__title {
    font-size: 14px;
    line-height: 1.7;
    font-weight: 600;
  }

  .policy__meta {
    color: var(--ink-2);
  }

  /* Muted chrome links in the meta line — underlined, never colour-only. */
  .policy__meta a {
    color: inherit;
    text-decoration: underline;
    text-decoration-color: var(--rule-strong);
    text-underline-offset: 0.15em;
  }

  .policy__meta a:hover {
    color: var(--ink);
    text-decoration-color: currentColor;
  }
</style>
